-- #1216, ARM 1 — cross-module FIELD-NAME collision at different indices.
-- `beta` is index 1 in this module's `Rec`, index 0 in `other.mdk`'s `Other`.
-- `medaka run` (interpreter) was always correct: it resolves fields by NAME
-- at run time, so it is structurally immune to slot/index questions. The
-- NATIVE EMITTER read the wrong slot for `.field` access once it had also
-- seen another module's record sharing that field name at a different
-- index — `getBeta` on a genuine `Rec` came back with Other's `alpha`
-- (index 1, where `beta` sits in `Rec`) instead of `Rec`'s own `beta`.
--
-- Re-pointed from the drained must-fail pin
-- `test/must_fail_fixtures/1216-xmod-record-field-name-collision/` (both arms
-- of #1216 confirmed fixed 2026-08-17; see that commit's message). This is a
-- BUILD+EXECUTE-graded regression, not a check/run one: `check` is clean and
-- both `run` and `build` themselves exit 0 in every state of this bug — only
-- the EXECUTED binary's printed value ever carried the defect, silently, at
-- exit 0. That is exactly what `diff_compiler_llvm_modules.sh` grades: it
-- emits via the native backend, links, EXECUTES the real binary, and diffs
-- against a golden captured independently of the built binary.
--
-- Expected output, derived BY HAND from the language semantics (field access
-- resolves by declaration, not layout) and cross-checked against the
-- interpreter, which is unaffected by this bug class: `ob` then `BBB`.
import other.{Other(..)}

data Rec = Rec { alpha : String, beta : String }

getBeta : Rec -> String
getBeta r = r.beta

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (Other { beta = "ob", alpha = "oa" }.beta)
  putStrLn (getBeta (Rec { alpha = "AAA", beta = "BBB" }))
